# GitHub & Git strategy (agent must follow)

**SSOT docs (read when unsure):** `docs/BRANCHING_STRATEGY.md`, `docs/VERSIONING_POLICY.md`, `docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md`

The product owner is not a programmer. After major or fundamental work (or a coherent series of related changes), **you must commit and push** using this strategy—do not leave durable progress uncommitted unless the user says to wait.

## When to commit and push (timing)

Do this automatically when any of these are true:

1. A feature/fix/chore slice is **functionally complete** (builds, hooks pass, intent delivered).
2. A **series of related edits** forms one logical unit (e.g. DI triage + migrations + i18n).
3. Work is **risky to lose** (schema, IPC, auth, migrations, multi-file refactors).
4. The user asks to save/push progress, or ends a major task without an open “hold commits” request.

Do **not** spam micro-commits for every tiny edit mid-debug; batch into one conventional commit per logical unit. Prefer **one clear commit** over many noisy ones for the same slice.

## Branch model (never violate)

```
feature|fix|chore → develop (PR) → staging → main (PR)
hotfix from release tag on main → PR to main + PR to develop
```

- Work only on `feature/*`, `fix/*`, `chore/*`, or `hotfix/*` — never commit directly to `main`, `staging`, or `develop`.
- If detached/wrong branch: create/switch to the correct branch **before** committing; never rewrite protected history.
- Keep existing feature branch when already on one (e.g. `feature/p5-sp00-…`); do not invent a parallel branch for the same sprint unless asked.

## Commit message style (unified conventional)

Format:

```text
type(scope): short imperative summary

- optional body bullets: why / what modules
```

| type | use for |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | new capability |
| `fix` | bug fix |
| `chore` | gitignore, deps, tooling, non-app docs |
| `docs` | documentation only |
| `refactor` | no behavior change |
| `test` | tests only |
| `perf` | performance |

- Scope = module area: `di`, `org`, `auth`, `admin`, `wo`, `gitignore`, `phase5`, etc.
- Subject: imperative, ≤ ~72 chars, no trailing period.
- Match recent repo style (e.g. `feat(di,lookups,auth): …`, `chore(gitignore): …`).

## Safe commit procedure (mandatory checklist)

1. `git status` + `git diff` + `git log -5` (style check).
2. Stage **only source of truth**: app code, migrations, shared IPC, docs intended for the change.
3. **Never stage:** secrets (`.env`, credentials), `node_modules/`, `src-tauri/target/`, `src-tauri/target-*`, `target-check*`, build caches, IDE junk, large generated binaries unless the user explicitly wants them.
4. If huge untracked trees appear under `target*` / `target-check*`: **do not** `git add -A`; stage paths explicitly; recommend/add `.gitignore` entries in a separate `chore(gitignore): …` commit.
5. Commit with HEREDOC/`-m` multi-line conventional message (PowerShell-safe on Windows).
6. If pre-commit/lint-staged fails: **fix the issue**, create a **new** commit — never `--no-verify`, never amend a failed commit.
7. `git push -u origin HEAD` (or push current feature branch). **No force-push** to `main`/`staging`/`develop`. No force-push elsewhere unless the user explicitly orders it.
8. Confirm clean tree for intended files; report commit SHA + branch + remote result.

## PRs, tags, releases

- Prefer an open PR `feature/*` → `develop` (draft OK). Update existing PR; do not open duplicates.
- **Annotated `v*` tags only on `main`** after the promotion path. Never tag feature/develop/staging tips as official releases.
- Version bumps (`package.json`, `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`, `CHANGELOG.md`) only when doing a real release/versioning task — same commit, identical strings.
- `git push origin --tags` only when new tags exist and policy allows them (on `main`).

## Hard forbidden actions

| Action | Status |
|--------|--------|
| Direct commit to `main` / `staging` / `develop` | Forbidden |
| Force-push protected branches | Never |
| Amend/rebase **pushed** shared commits | Forbidden |
| `--no-verify` / skip hooks | Forbidden |
| `reset --hard` / destructive discard of user work | Forbidden without explicit user order + backup |
| Commit secrets or build artifact trees | Forbidden |
| Official release tags off `main` | Forbidden |

## End-of-task report (always)

After auto commit/push, tell the user briefly:

- commit SHA + message
- branch pushed
- PR link if any
- what was excluded (e.g. local `target-check*`)
- if a release tag was **not** created, say why (tags only on `main`)

```text
❌ BAD — leave a finished multi-file feature uncommitted; or git add -A including target-check
✅ GOOD — conventional commit of the logical unit on feature/*, push, keep/update PR to develop
```
